  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea is to use a genetic algorithm (GA) to explore the space of step functions that minimize the maximum overlap  $C_5$. The GA will search over continuous step functions represented as vectors of 80 points, satisfying the integral constraint $\\int_0^2 h(x) dx = 1$ (sum $h[i] * dx = 1$). This continuous representation allows for better optimization and repair of constraints compared to discrete step functions.\n\nThe key bottleneck is the high dimensionality ($80$). However, with a local search strategy and dimension reduction, the search space can be navigated efficiently within the time budget. The algorithm starts from the given initial solution and uses GA with repair operators to minimize the maximum overlap.\n\nKey limitations include:\n1. Simple random initialization may yield solutions without optimization\n2. GA requires many evaluations \n3. Repair operation must maintain integral constraint\n\nThe GA is implemented with:\n- Population: 50 individuals at start, then grows\n- Crossover and mutation operators with repair\n- Tournament selection\n- Dynamic population size adjustment based on progress\n- Dimension reduction path if progress plateaus\n\nJob done for all implementation-relevant details below.",
